<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="UTP and CTP dephosphorylation II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7177" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; The key enzyme of this pathway is apyrase (|FRAME: EC-3.6.1.5|). Apyrases are active against both di- and triphosphate nucleotides (NDPs and NTPs) and hydrolyze NTPs to nucleotide monophosphates (NMPs) in two distinct successive phosphate-releasing steps, with NDPs as intermediates. They are widely distributed in eukaryotic cells from yeast |CITS: [10409709]| to mammals |CITS: [9556635]|, but similar enzymes have also been reported in bacteria |CITS: [15576788]|. They differ from ATPases, which specifically hydrolyze ATP, by hydrolyzing both ATP and ADP (although the relative rates of ATP and ADP hydrolysis differ depending on the species) |CITS: [8611638]|. Apyrases can be divided into ecto-apyrases, in which the catalytic domain is outside the cell |CITS: [7721538]|, and endo-apyrases, with the catalytic site inside the cell |CITS: [8611638]|. Ecto-apyrases participate in neurotransmission, blood platelet aggregation, and pressure regulation. Endo-apyrases are involved in protein glycosylation and sugar level control and in regulation of membrane integrity. Apyrases are well studied in lower |CITS: [8703025][10409709][12686557]| and higher eukaryotes |CITS: [9364474][11985621][15122917][16752921]|, and less so in plants |CITS: [8579614][Steinebrunner00][17434987]|. They appear to be absent from bacteria and archaea. Apyrases are involved in many functions, and the pathway may be utilized in different capacities depending on specific cases. One case that has been studied involves protein and lipid glycosylation within the Golgi apparatus. NDP-sugars are synthesized in the cytosol and translocated into the Golgi lumen via specific membrane carriers. After transfer of sugar residues to proteins and lipids by galactosyltransferases, the resulting NDPs is likely hydrolyzed by the apyrases to NMPs. In this way, the NDPs, which are highly inhibitory to galactosyltransferases, do not accumulate in the lumen of the Golgi apparatus. The NMPs then exit the Golgi lumen by exchange with new cytosolic NDP-sugars via an anti-porter |CITS: [9204706][10409709]|.</Description>
